// A cautionary example (phase 7, O): deriving a query index from a challenge
// in-circuit — and why the determinacy type system is not enough here.
//
// The verifier wants the query index to be the low bits of the Fiat-Shamir
// challenge, so the prover cannot choose which positions to open. The natural
// binding is `challenge = index + domain*high` with the two index bits pinned:
//
//   determinacy PROVES `idx` determined — it is a function of the inputs.
//
// But it is NOT sound. `high` is unconstrained, so for ANY index in {0,1,2,3}
// the prover can pick high = (challenge - index)/domain and satisfy every
// constraint. The index is not bound to the challenge at all. Soundness needs
// `high` range-checked (a full bit decomposition of the challenge), which needs
// a decomposition hint the language does not yet provide. This file exists to
// make that gap concrete; see backend/zkc-core/tests/index_binding_tests.rs.
gadget derive_index(challenge: field, b0: field, b1: field, high: field) -> (idx: field) {
    assert b0 * (1 - b0) == 0;
    assert b1 * (1 - b1) == 0;
    let index = b0 + 2 * b1;
    assert challenge == index + 4 * high;
    assert idx == index;
}

circuit IndexFromChallenge {
    private challenge: field;
    private b0: field;
    private b1: field;
    private high: field;
    output idx: field;
    (idx) = derive_index(challenge, b0, b1, high);
}